      "rev_n_1_78e9e3_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_78e9e3_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_78e9e3",
        "thought_text": "Sort both students and bugs by their respective abilities and complexities. Assign the simplest bug to the least capable student who can fix it, ensuring the total passes do not exceed s. This greedy approach might not yield the optimal solution but provides a straightforward starting point.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794409273.304737
      },
      "rev_n_2_164e81_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_164e81_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_164e81",
        "thought_text": "Use dynamic programming to create a table where each entry represents the minimum number of days required to fix bugs up to a certain index using a subset of students, while keeping track of the total passes used. This approach ensures optimality but may face challenges due to the large input sizes.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794409273.304812
      },
      "rev_n_3_1702f8_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_1702f8_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_1702f8",
        "thought_text": "Implement a binary search on the number of days required to fix all bugs. For each candidate number of days, check if it's possible to assign bugs to students without exceeding the pass limit s. This method balances between efficiency and optimality.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794409273.3048162
      },
      "rev_n_4_ede3fa_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_ede3fa_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_ede3fa",
        "thought_text": "Instead of using a greedy approach, consider a dynamic programming (DP) solution where we define a DP table dp[i][j] representing the minimum number of days needed to fix the first i bugs using the first j students, while ensuring the total passes do not exceed s. This approach might be more complex but could potentially find an optimal solution.",
